✅ CS6P05 Project Proposal
Project Title:
Design and Implementation of a Cyber Essentials Compliance Readiness Framework for Small and Medium Enterprises (SMEs)
(Commercial name: Tanasiom Aegis Cyber Readiness Service)
Student: Dumitru Tanasie (dut0032@my.londonmet.ac.uk)
Student ID: 22041369
Course: BSc Computer Networking & Cyber Security
Supervisor: Dr Subeksha Shrestha
________________________________________
1 Introduction / Problem Statement
Small and medium enterprises (SMEs) are increasingly required to demonstrate cybersecurity compliance when bidding for government contracts or handling personal data.
The UK Government’s Cyber Essentials (CE) scheme provides a recognised baseline for information-security assurance (NCSC 2023).
However, many SMEs face challenges such as limited expertise, lack of awareness of compliance requirements, and absence of structured processes for implementation (Small Business Commissioner 2023).
These issues lead to failed tenders, data-breach exposure and non-compliance penalties.
A guided Cyber Essentials readiness framework is therefore needed to help SMEs reach compliance efficiently.
________________________________________
2 Aim of the Project
To design, develop and evaluate a Cyber Essentials Readiness Toolkit that assists SMEs in understanding, preparing for, and attaining Cyber Essentials certification securely and efficiently.
________________________________________
3 Objectives
1.	Research Cyber Essentials requirements and common SME security challenges (NCSC 2023; IASME 2023).
2.	Design a structured readiness and self-assessment workflow aligned with CE control areas.
3.	Develop a practical toolkit including:
 • Risk-assessment and device-control templates
 • Implementation guidance
 • Policy and procedure pack
4.	Prototype a lightweight web dashboard to visualise readiness status.
5.	Evaluate the toolkit using a simulated SME case study.
6.	Reflect on the legal, ethical and professional implications of compliance (BCS 2023).
________________________________________
4 Background / Rationale
The project aligns with the BSc Computer Networking & Cyber Security programme learning outcomes and with industry best practice.
Cyber Essentials is a UK Government-backed standard required for many public-sector suppliers and recommended by insurers (NCSC 2023).
Research shows that SMEs struggle to interpret technical CE controls, often resulting in partial or misapplied implementations (Von Solms & Van Niekerk 2013).
Producing an accessible, step-by-step readiness toolkit therefore fills an identified gap in SME cyber-governance capability (Small Business Commissioner 2023).
________________________________________
5 Proposed Solution / Artefact
Component	Description
Initial Security Assessment Questionnaire	Evaluate baseline security posture
System Inventory & Access Control Template	Map devices, users and permissions
Firewall / MFA / Patch Guides	Step-by-step configuration and verification
Compliance Gap Report Template	Summarise readiness and remediation steps
Policy Documentation Pack	Password, Remote Access and Incident Response policies
Monitoring & Review Checklist	Maintain ongoing CE compliance
Together these form the Tanasiom Aegis Cyber Readiness Toolkit, providing a reusable compliance-readiness framework for SMEs.
________________________________________
6 Methodology
An investigative and design-based methodology will be applied (Dawson 2015).
Stage	Activities
Research & Literature Review	Analyse CE documentation and SME threat landscape (NCSC 2023)
Requirements Analysis	Identify SME technical environments and constraints
Framework Design	Create readiness workflow and artefact architecture
Artefact Development	Produce templates, policies and dashboard prototype
Evaluation	Test toolkit using a simulated SME case study
Reflection & Reporting	Analyse results and compile final report (Dawson 2015)
This process supports learning outcomes LO1 – LO7:
independent research → design → implementation → evaluation → professional reflection.
________________________________________
7 Resource Requirements
•	Software: Microsoft 365, Visual Studio Code, Draw.io, GitHub.
•	Documentation: NCSC and IASME official guides.
•	Hardware: Standard laptop environment for simulation.
No external datasets, paid hosting or company data are required.
